      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "SFRM1000",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 313,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p13_b9",
      "text": "Interestingly, pre-fine-tuning CLIPSurgery on the target dataset improves its\nstandalone mask quality but leads to worse final performance after joint training\n(Table 8). Although the pre-adapted model starts from a stronger initialization,\nit converges to a substantially lower final accuracy (67.5 mIoU vs. 78.3), sug-\ngesting that strong prior specialization can hinder SAM\u2013CLIP co-adaptation and\nmay even underperform weaker semantic guides such as standard CLIP (72.8)\nwhen integrated into the joint system. This suggests that maximizing the stan-\ndalone performance of a vision\u2013language model does not necessarily translate\nto better segmentation performance when the model is later integrated into a\njointly trained system.\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        356.0,
        480.61,
        473.56
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        1423,
        1922,
        1894
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "SFRM1000",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 730,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p13_b10",
      "text": "Overall, these results indicate that effective semantic conditioning emerges\nfrom joint optimization rather than from independently maximizing the quality\nof the semantic encoder.\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        475.55,
        480.61,
        509.42
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
